 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "o1",
   "text": "She won an Oscar. And Hollywood still would not let her be buried in its cemetery.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "mammy",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.5,
     1.58
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.75,
     0.45
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "She won an Oscar. *Hollywood still wouldn't let her be buried there.*"
   },
   "captions": false
  },
  {
   "id": "o2",
   "text": "On the night of February 29th, 1940, Hollywood's biggest stars gathered at the Ambassador Hotel in Los Angeles, for the Academy Awards.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "grove",
    "caption": "The Cocoanut Grove, Ambassador Hotel"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o3",
   "text": "One nominee did not sit with the rest of her film's stars. She sat at a small table, against the far wall.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "h1941",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.2
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o4",
   "text": "Then they read out her name. She had just become the first Black person ever to win an Oscar.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "porch",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o5",
   "text": "Twelve years later, she asked for one last thing: to be buried in Hollywood Cemetery. The answer was no.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Whites only",
    "sub": "Hollywood Cemetery, 1952"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o6",
   "text": "Her name was Hattie McDaniel. This is her story.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Hattie McDaniel",
    "sub": "the first Black Oscar winner"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e1",
   "text": "Hattie McDaniel was born in Wichita, Kansas, in the 1890s, the youngest of thirteen children.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "studio",
    "focus": [
     0.47,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.65,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Wichita, Kansas"
    }
   },
   "section": "early"
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "text": "Her parents had both been born into slavery. Her father, Henry, had fought for the Union in the Civil War.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "usct",
    "caption": "Black Union soldiers, Civil War"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e3",
   "text": "In the early 1900s, the family moved to Colorado, and settled in Denver.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "denver",
    "caption": "Sixteenth Street, Denver"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e4",
   "text": "Hattie loved to perform. At East Denver High School, she won a gold medal in a recitation contest, for a poem called Convict Joe.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Gold medal",
    "sub": "East Denver High School"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5",
   "text": "Her father and her brothers started a minstrel show, and soon she was on the road with them, singing and dancing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "laundry",
    "focus": [
     0.42,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e6",
   "text": "She and her sister Etta even started a show of their own.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The McDaniel Sisters",
    "sub": "Denver, 1914"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e7",
   "text": "In the 1920s, she toured with George Morrison's orchestra, and sang on Denver radio. She is often called the first Black woman to sing on American radio.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "beulah48",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.28
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.62,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "text": "Then her luck ran out. A touring show she was in went broke, and she ended up stranded in Milwaukee.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Stranded",
    "sub": "Milwaukee, Wisconsin"
   },
   "section": "stranded"
  },
  {
   "id": "s1b",
   "text": "She was far from home, with no show, and no money.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "yard",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "text": "The only work she could find was at a nightclub outside the city, Sam Pick's Club Madrid. Not on the stage. In the washroom, as an attendant.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Washroom attendant",
    "sub": "Club Madrid, Milwaukee"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "text": "Black performers were not allowed on that stage. She talked the owner into letting her sing anyway, and she became a regular.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "laundry2",
    "focus": [
     0.42,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "text": "In 1931, she moved to Los Angeles to try the movies. Her brother Sam and her sister Etta were actors too.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "cbs",
    "caption": "Hollywood, 1930s"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h1",
   "text": "Hollywood had one kind of part for her, again and again. The maid. The cook. The mammy.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "kitchen",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "section": "hollywood"
  },
  {
   "id": "h2",
   "text": "In 1934, the director John Ford cast her in Judge Priest, opposite Will Rogers. It was her first major role.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "porch",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "lowerThird": {
     "name": "Judge Priest",
     "sub": "1934"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h3",
   "text": "She even sang a duet with Rogers.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "yard",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h4",
   "text": "Bigger parts followed, including The Little Colonel, with Shirley Temple.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "ladies",
    "focus": [
     0.27,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h4b",
   "text": "In film after film, she stole scenes from the stars, with a look, a line, or a song.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "laundry2",
    "focus": [
     0.42,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h5",
   "text": "She would appear in hundreds of films. In most of them, her name was not even on the screen.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "sacred",
    "focus": [
     0.24,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "lowerThird": {
     "name": "Nothing Sacred",
     "sub": "1937"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h6",
   "text": "When people criticized her for the roles she took, her reported answer became famous.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "kitchen2",
    "focus": [
     0.35,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "h7",
   "text": "Why should I complain about making seven hundred dollars a week playing a maid? If I didn't, I'd be making seven dollars a week being one.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "quote",
    "quote": "Why should I complain about making $700 a week playing a maid? If I didn't, I'd be making $7 a week being one.",
    "by": "Hattie McDaniel"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g1",
   "text": "In 1939, the producer David Selznick was casting Gone with the Wind.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "poster",
    "caption": "Gone with the Wind, 1939"
   },
   "section": "gwtw"
  },
  {
   "id": "g2",
   "text": "Hattie McDaniel won the part of Mammy, the house servant of Scarlett O'Hara's family.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "gwtw",
    "focus": [
     0.14,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.5,
     1.6
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.4,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g3",
   "text": "The film opened in Atlanta on December 15th, 1939, at Loew's Grand Theatre.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "premiere",
    "caption": "Loew's Grand Theatre, Atlanta, 1939"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "15 December 1939"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g3b",
   "text": "It should have been one of the biggest nights of her career.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "stairs",
    "focus": [
     0.25,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g4",
   "text": "Hattie McDaniel was not there. Under Georgia's segregation laws, the theatre was for white audiences only. None of the film's Black actors could go.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "premiere",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.22
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g5",
   "text": "Her co-star Clark Gable was so angry, he threatened to boycott the premiere. She convinced him to go anyway.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "poster",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.15
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.6,
     1.7
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g6",
   "text": "In the South, the studio even cut the Black actors out of the film's publicity.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "jet2",
    "caption": "Jet magazine, 1952",
    "crop": [
     0,
     0.5,
     1,
     0.98
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "text": "Then came the nominations. Hattie McDaniel was nominated for Best Supporting Actress. No Black actor had ever been nominated before.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "mammy",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ]
   },
   "section": "oscar"
  },
  {
   "id": "a1b",
   "text": "Her co-star Olivia de Havilland was nominated in the very same category, for the very same film.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "gwtw",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.25
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.3,
     1.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "text": "The ceremony was held at the Cocoanut Grove, in the Ambassador Hotel. The hotel did not normally admit Black guests.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "grove2",
    "caption": "The Cocoanut Grove, Ambassador Hotel"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a3",
   "text": "She was let in. But she and her escort were seated at a separate table, on the far side of the room, away from her co-stars.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "grove2",
    "focus": [
     0.8,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.3,
     1.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a4",
   "text": "She wore white gardenias in her hair.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "h1941",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.15
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.3,
     1.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a5",
   "text": "Then the winner was announced. Hattie McDaniel.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "laundry2",
    "focus": [
     0.42,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "29 February 1940"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a6",
   "text": "She said: I sincerely hope I shall always be a credit to my race, and to the motion picture industry.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "quote",
    "quote": "I sincerely hope I shall always be a credit to my race and to the motion picture industry.",
    "by": "Hattie McDaniel, 1940"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a7",
   "text": "Her award was not the famous golden statuette. Supporting winners then received a small plaque.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "jet1",
    "caption": "Hattie McDaniel and her Oscar plaque",
    "crop": [
     0,
     0,
     1,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a8",
   "text": "It would be half a century before another Black woman won an acting Oscar.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "50 years",
    "sub": "until the next Black woman won"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "text": "Not everyone celebrated. The NAACP, and its leader Walter White, criticized her for playing maids and mammies, roles that kept racist stereotypes alive.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "kitchen2",
    "focus": [
     0.35,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "section": "critics"
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "text": "She kept working. During World War Two, she led the Negro Division of the Hollywood Victory Committee, bringing entertainment to Black soldiers.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "victory",
    "caption": "Hattie McDaniel (center), Hollywood Victory Committee"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k1",
   "text": "By then she owned a large house in West Adams, a Los Angeles neighborhood so popular with Black stars that it was nicknamed Sugar Hill.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "h1941",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ]
   },
   "section": "home"
  },
  {
   "id": "k2",
   "text": "In 1945, some white neighbors went to court. They wanted to use old racial covenants to force the Black homeowners out.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Sugar Hill",
    "sub": "Los Angeles, 1945"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1945"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k3",
   "text": "Hattie McDaniel helped rally her neighbors to fight back. The judge threw the case out.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "ladies",
    "focus": [
     0.27,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k4",
   "text": "Three years later, the Supreme Court ruled that courts could not enforce racial covenants anywhere in the country.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1948",
    "sub": "covenants unenforceable"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b1",
   "text": "In 1947, she took over the lead in a radio comedy, The Beulah Show.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "beulah48",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.25,
     1.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.62,
     0.42
    ]
   },
   "section": "beulah"
  },
  {
   "id": "b2",
   "text": "Later, she began playing Beulah on television too. But she managed only six episodes. She was ill with breast cancer.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "beulah51",
    "focus": [
     0.6,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.6,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "b3",
   "text": "The role went to Louise Beavers, another actress who lived on Sugar Hill.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "beulah48",
    "focus": [
     0.45,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d1",
   "text": "Hattie McDaniel died on October 26th, 1952. She was 59.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "studio",
    "focus": [
     0.47,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.15,
     1.25
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "26 October 1952"
   },
   "section": "end"
  },
  {
   "id": "d2",
   "text": "Some three thousand mourners came to her funeral.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "stairs",
    "focus": [
     0.25,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d2b",
   "text": "But Hollywood had one more insult left for her.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "One last wish",
    "sub": "1952"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d3",
   "text": "In her will, she made one request. She wanted to be buried in Hollywood Cemetery.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Her last wish",
    "sub": "Hollywood Cemetery"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d4",
   "text": "The cemetery refused. It did not bury Black people.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Refused",
    "sub": "whites only"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d5",
   "text": "She was buried across town, at Rosedale Cemetery.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "rosedale",
    "caption": "Rosedale Cemetery, Los Angeles"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d6",
   "text": "She left her Oscar plaque to Howard University, the historically Black university in Washington.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "jet1",
    "caption": "Jet magazine, November 1952",
    "crop": [
     0,
     0,
     1,
     0.62
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d7",
   "text": "It was displayed there for years. Then, around 1970, it disappeared. It has never been found.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "jet1",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.18
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.8,
     1.95
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l1",
   "text": "In 1999, the cemetery that refused her had a new owner. He offered to move her there. Her family said no.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1999",
    "sub": "Hollywood Forever"
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "l2",
   "text": "So the cemetery put up a memorial to her instead. It stands there today.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "porch",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.32
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.32
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.16
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l3",
   "text": "She has two stars on the Hollywood Walk of Fame, one for film, and one for radio.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "star",
    "caption": "Hollywood Walk of Fame"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l4",
   "text": "In 2010, when Mo'Nique won the same Oscar, she wore a blue dress and a gardenia in her hair, as Hattie McDaniel had seventy years before.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "monique",
    "caption": "Mo'Nique, 2010"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l5",
   "text": "And in 2023, the Academy gave Howard University a replacement for her lost Oscar. The ceremony was called Hattie's Come Home.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Hattie's Come Home",
    "sub": "Howard University, 2023"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z1",
   "text": "Her parents were born into slavery. She cleaned washrooms. She played maids in hundreds of films.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "laundry",
    "focus": [
     0.42,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "section": "close"
  },
  {
   "id": "z2",
   "text": "And she won Hollywood's highest honor, at a table set apart from everyone else.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "mammy",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z2b",
   "text": "Her Oscar is still missing. But her name is not.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "star",
    "caption": "Hollywood Walk of Fame"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z3",
   "text": "That was Hattie McDaniel. Subscribe for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
